  "outcome": "needs_user_info",
  "reply_to_user": "Hi Leo, I found the likely cause. Your laptop's drive is almost full: only about 9 GB of its 256 GB is free (3.5%). Windows slows down a lot when free space drops below about 10%. Please try these steps:\n1. Empty the Recycle Bin and clear out your Downloads folder.\n2. Go to Settings > System > Storage > Temporary files and remove the temporary files and old Windows Update files.\n3. In OneDrive settings, turn on Files On-Demand so your synced files stay in the cloud until you open them.\n4. Uninstall any apps you no longer use.\n5. Do a full restart (Start > Power > Restart). Closing the lid isn't enough.\n6. Install any pending Windows updates, then restart again.\nOnce you're done, please reply and let me know: (a) whether it's faster, (b) when you last fully restarted before this, and (c) whether the slowness started after an update or a new app install. I'll check the free space from my side to confirm. If it's still slow after cleanup, I'll pass it to our next-level team to look at the hardware.",
  "internal_note": "User Leo Martins (id 6, Sales). Laptop BL-LT-0106 is assigned to him, Win11 Pro, online, last check-in 2026-10-09 14:10 UTC. Device status shows disk 256 GB with only 9 GB free (3.5%) and a low disk space warning. That is well under the 10% threshold in KB-017 and is the likely cause of the slowness (KB-019 quick check 1). I sent the user the KB-017 cleanup steps and the KB-019 restart/updates steps, and asked for last restart time and any recent update or install (KB-019 quick checks 2-3). Next step: re-run check_device_status after the user replies. If it's still under 10% free after cleanup, or still slow after restart, updates and cleanup, escalate to Tier 2 per KB-017/KB-019 (possible larger drive or replacement). No action tools used.",
